WEBVTT

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.940
Operationalizing threat intelligence means turning it into defensive action.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.650
A report that sits unread in a portal does not improve security by itself.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:12.080
CTI becomes useful when it changes a workflow: a detection is built, a hunt is run,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.030
an incident is scoped more accurately,

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:18.240
a vulnerability is prioritized, an alert is enriched, an exposure is reduced,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.605
a control is validated, or a leader makes a better risk decision.

00:00:21.605 --> 00:00:24.740
Detection engineering is one of the most common operational uses.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:29.150
Intelligence about adversary behavior can become detection logic, telemetry requirements,

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.170
test cases, and false-positive notes.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:35.345
The strongest detections often focus on behavior rather than a single brittle indicator.

00:00:35.345 --> 00:00:38.770
CTI can help detection teams understand why a behavior matters,

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:42.290
where it appears in the attack chain, which platforms should produce telemetry,

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.385
and which response action should follow an alert.

00:00:44.385 --> 00:00:46.530
Threat hunting also benefits from CTI.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.330
A hunt should begin with a hypothesis,

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.985
such as whether a behavior described in recent reporting exists in the environment.

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:55.320
The analyst can use ATT&CK mappings, incident lessons,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.325
and internal telemetry to shape the hunt.

00:00:57.325 --> 00:00:58.480
If the hunt finds nothing,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.715
that result may still be useful if the scope and telemetry are clear.

00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:04.325
It can validate controls or reveal visibility gaps.

00:01:04.325 --> 00:01:08.225
Incident response teams use CTI to scope faster and ask better questions.

00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:12.705
Intelligence can suggest related behaviors to search for, likely follow-on activity,

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:15.375
affected platforms, and evidence that should be preserved.

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:16.900
It can also prevent tunnel vision.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.195
If a single IOC triggered the investigation,

00:01:19.195 --> 00:01:23.510
CTI can help responders look beyond that one artifact and consider the broader pattern of

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:23.980
behavior,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.980
timing, infrastructure, and objectives.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.325
CTI improves vulnerability prioritization by adding evidence of exploitation,

00:01:30.325 --> 00:01:34.845
adversary interest, affected sectors, exposed assets, and compensating controls.

00:01:34.845 --> 00:01:37.525
Not every critical score means the same operational risk,

00:01:37.525 --> 00:01:39.475
and not every lower score is safe to ignore.

00:01:39.475 --> 00:01:43.520
Intelligence helps vulnerability teams decide what to patch first, what to monitor,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.195
what to isolate, and what to explain to risk owners.

00:01:46.195 --> 00:01:49.740
The same principle applies to incident response scoping, alert enrichment,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.185
exposure management, and executive risk reporting.

00:01:52.185 --> 00:01:56.035
Operationalization requires ownership, workflow integration, and measurement.

00:01:56.035 --> 00:01:57.120
Who receives the intelligence?

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.170
Who turns it into a ticket or detection?

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.220
How is completion tracked?

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.240
How are false positives reported back?

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.380
What outcome shows value?

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.325
Metrics can include detections created, hunts completed, alerts enriched,

00:02:07.325 --> 00:02:10.275
vulnerabilities reprioritized, incidents scoped faster,

00:02:10.275 --> 00:02:12.415
or decisions made with clearer confidence.

00:02:12.415 --> 00:02:14.915
Unused intelligence is just stored information.

00:02:14.915 --> 00:02:17.565
Operationalized intelligence becomes part of the defense.

00:02:17.565 --> 00:02:18.950
Feedback is the maintenance loop.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.550
If a detection was noisy, CTI should learn why.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.115
If a hunt lacked telemetry, that gap should be recorded.

00:02:24.115 --> 00:02:27.960
If a vulnerability priority changed because exploitation evidence was weak,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.440
the rationale should be reusable.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.530
Operationalization is not finished when intelligence is delivered.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.690
It is finished when the workflow outcome is understood.
